    "INDEX-1<SEP>explain what the theory of sexual selection is and give an example.<SEP>The theory of sexual selection is a concept in evolutionary biology that explains the evolution of traits that are advantageous in attracting mates, rather than in survival. Sexual selection occurs when individuals with certain traits are more likely to mate and pass on those traits to their offspring. An example of sexual selection is the bright plumage of male peacocks, which is believed to be a result of sexual selection. The bright colors attract mates, even though they may make the male more visible to predators.": -1.828125,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>explain what the theory of sexual selection is and give an example.<SEP>The theory of sexual selection is a concept in evolutionary biology that explains the evolution of traits that are advantageous in attracting mates, rather than those that are advantageous for survival. This theory was first proposed by Charles Darwin in his book \"The Descent of Man, and Selection in Relation to Sex.\" Sexual selection can lead to the evolution of traits such as bright colors, elaborate courtship behaviors, and intricate structures that are not directly related to an organism's ability to survive in its environment. \n\nAn example of sexual selection is the evolution of the peacock's elaborate train of feathers. While the train may make the peacock more conspicuous to predators and reduce its survival chances, it is believed to be a signal of fitness to potential mates. Peahens prefer to mate with peacocks with larger and more colorful trains, and this preference has led to the evolution of the extravagant plumage seen in peacocks today.": 1.578125,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>explain what the theory of sexual selection is and give an example.<SEP>Sexual selection is a theory that explains the evolution of traits that are advantageous in attracting mates, rather than those that improve survival. It was proposed by Charles Darwin in his book \"On the Origin of Species\" and further developed in his work \"The Descent of Man, and Selection in Relation to Sex.\"\n\nSexual selection can occur through two mechanisms: intrasexual selection and intersexual selection. Intrasexual selection occurs when individuals compete with members of the same sex for mates, leading to the evolution of traits that give an advantage in competition. Intersexual selection occurs when members of the opposite sex prefer certain traits in potential mates, leading to the evolution of those traits in the population.\n\nAn example of sexual selection is the peacock's tail. The peacock's tail is a complex and elaborate structure that is energetically costly to maintain. It is believed that the tail evolved through sexual selection because it serves as a signal of the peacock's fitness to potential mates. Females prefer males with larger and more elaborate tails, which indicates that the male is in better health and has access to more resources. This preference leads to the evolution of the trait in the population.": 1.4296875,
